WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.412 --> 00:00:12.620
PETER BLUM: Hi, everyone,
and welcome to our session,

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.530
Innovations in DDoS,
WAF, Firewalls,

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:18.260
and Network-based
Threat Detection.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:19.520
My name is Peter Blum.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:22.790
And I run the network security
PM team here at Google Cloud.

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:26.477
And I'm joined by Emil, who I'm
going to let introduce himself.

00:00:26.477 --> 00:00:27.560
EMIL KINER: Thanks, Peter.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:28.060
Hi.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:29.030
My name is Emil Kiner.

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:32.000
And I run product management
for our DDoS mitigation and web

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.677
application firewall product
line called Cloud Armor.

00:00:36.677 --> 00:00:38.510
PETER BLUM: So today,
I'm going to start off

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:40.970
by sharing our vision
for network security

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:43.873
and provide a brief
overview of our solutions.

00:00:43.873 --> 00:00:45.290
After that, I'm
going to hand over

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:46.760
to Emil, who's
going to get us up

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.190
to date on all the
latest with Cloud Armor.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:52.018
And then I'll pick back up and
cover all the great new things

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:53.810
that we've been doing
in network protection

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:59.930
with our Cloud firewall,
NAT, and IDS offerings.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:03.020
So let me start with our broader
vision of network security

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:04.069
at Google Cloud.

00:01:04.069 --> 00:01:05.750
As you can see in
our vision statement,

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:08.930
our goal with network security
is to make it invisible.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:10.970
We want to weave it
into the cloud fabric

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:15.530
with automated, but verifiable,
protection, simplified control,

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:18.110
and clear prioritization
of critical threats

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:19.790
with relevant context.

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:22.672
And where we can, we also
want to provide the solution.

00:01:22.672 --> 00:01:24.380
Emil is actually going
to talk about some

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.810
of the new capabilities in
Cloud Armor in just a bit

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:28.940
that I think are a
perfect example of what

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:33.750
we're working towards
across the entire portfolio.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:36.660
I wanted to provide an overview
of our network security

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:37.440
portfolio.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.200
I'm going to overlay this on
a high-level diagram that's

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:45.150
showing flows in, between,
and out of our cloud.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.790
First, in terms of
handling incoming traffic

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:51.240
to internet-facing services,
we provide an integrated edge

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:55.140
delivery stack that includes
a global load balancer and CDN

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:58.650
that provide for availability,
scalability, and performance.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:00.780
And then built into
this stack to provide

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:04.680
our global-scale DDoS protection
and a great web application

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.870
firewall is Cloud
Armor, which Emil

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.949
is going to dig into shortly.

00:02:08.949 --> 00:02:10.770
And then we provide
defense in depth

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:14.220
with a network firewall and NAT
service built into our cloud

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:16.080
networking fabric.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.780
These same two
services also provide

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:22.050
for protection and privacy
for traffic out of the network

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:23.670
as well.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:26.450
And as you can expect, our
network security solutions

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.000
are integrated in our
Cloud Security products,

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.970
bringing in context about
threats, integration

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:34.520
with our bot, fraud, and
application access solutions

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:36.020
as well.

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:38.050
And then we provide
a full complement

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:40.540
of network-level
telemetry from metadata

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:43.990
about traffic flows inside
and out of the cloud, data

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:47.230
about traffic we block at the
network and application layer,

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:49.720
plus we provide full
packet inspection

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.540
services with our packet
mirroring capability.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:54.310
And with the next
few months, you'll

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:56.860
see we've also released
network threat detection.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:58.840
It's a system called
Cloud IDS that

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.840
was a joint partnership with
Palo Alto Networks, where

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:05.560
we use their threat detection
systems within our own cloud.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.260
All the alerts and telemetry
from these products

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:11.020
are also integrated into
our own threat-hunting,

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:14.960
alert management, and unified
security management services.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:17.560
And then we also integrate with
third-party SIEM and source

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:19.130
systems as well.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:21.430
So with that, let
me hand over to Emil

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:23.807
to take us through Cloud Armor.

00:03:23.807 --> 00:03:24.890
EMIL KINER: Thanks, Peter.

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:26.480
Today, I'm excited
to share with you

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.550
all the great new
developments and capabilities

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:32.150
we've released with Cloud
Armor over the past year.

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:34.640
Cloud Armor is Google Cloud's
DDoS protection service

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:36.500
and web application firewall.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:40.100
It provides global-scale defense
against volumetric, protocol,

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:41.750
and application-level attacks.

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:44.372
And it's built using the same
technology and infrastructure

00:03:44.372 --> 00:03:45.830
that we originally
built to protect

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:48.950
our own billion-user properties,
such as Search, Gmail,

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:50.278
and Maps.

00:03:50.278 --> 00:03:52.070
Cloud Armor reflects
our vision of bringing

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:55.040
to bear Google's global scale
to protect customer workloads

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:56.780
wherever they are deployed.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:58.460
Cloud Armor, in
addition to the rest

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:01.070
of our edge infrastructure,
including load balancing

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:04.370
and CDN, can be deployed
in front of your websites

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:06.140
or services, whether
you are running

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:09.020
on Google Cloud, on-prem, or
with another infrastructure

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:10.470
provider.

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:12.660
Over the past year, we've
been privileged enough

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:15.570
to help protect a growing
set of enterprise customers

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:17.970
while moving quickly to
expand our capabilities

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:20.399
and scope of protection,
all while investing

00:04:20.399 --> 00:04:23.280
heavily into building a
cutting-edge machine learning

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:26.753
solution to meet the evolving
threat landscape head-on.

00:04:26.753 --> 00:04:28.170
I'm excited to
share with you some

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:30.060
of our recent developments.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:32.950
Earlier this year, we introduced
Cloud Armor Managed Protection

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:35.760
Plus, a managed application
protection service

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:38.310
which bundles together all
the features and capabilities

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:41.370
of Cloud Armor with additional
value-added services

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:43.740
into an enterprise-friendly
and predictable

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:45.600
monthly subscription.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.720
Managed Protection Plus
delivers unrivaled application

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:51.000
protection while providing
the peace of mind

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.760
that Google has your back 24/7.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:55.920
In addition to including
all Cloud Armor WAF

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:58.980
rules, policies, and requests,
Managed Protection Plus

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:02.250
includes premium capabilities,
like Adaptive Protection,

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:05.940
as well as 24/7 DDoS response
support, which gives you

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:08.910
access to Google's DoS experts
to help mitigate attacks

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:10.950
targeting your services.

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:13.440
Further, with DDoS
Bill Protection,

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:15.840
we're removing the
financial risk of DDoS

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.140
by insulating you from spikes in
your networking expenses caused

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:21.570
by successful DDoS attacks.

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:23.250
Over the next several
quarters, you'll

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:26.550
see us continue to add
additional premium capabilities

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:31.740
and manage services into Managed
Protection Plus subscription.

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:34.980
Next, we want to talk about
our new Adaptive Protection

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:37.530
capability, which
has already generated

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:40.380
a lot of positive feedback
from a fast-growing enterprise

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:42.000
customer base.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.490
Adaptive Protection is a
machine learning-based solution

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:46.530
to detect and
efficiently respond

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:50.280
to application-level distributed
denial of service attacks.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:51.990
We have developed
Adaptive Protection

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:54.780
over the last several years
in close collaboration

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:56.760
with external and
internal customers,

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:59.910
like Project Shield,
continuously training

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:03.870
on attack data we observe
against all of Google.

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:07.320
Adaptive Protection develops
a per-application baseline

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:09.450
by learning what normal
traffic patterns look

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:12.030
like for each
protected application.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:13.980
In addition to
traffic volumes, we

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:16.290
assess dozens of
features and attributes

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:19.870
of the incoming request traffic
to establish the baseline.

00:06:19.870 --> 00:06:22.410
Next, Adaptive Protection
monitors traffic flows

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:24.510
in real time and
generates alerts

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:27.480
for anomalous or
potentially attack traffic.

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:29.280
A second set of
machine learning model

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:31.290
then distills the
anomalous traffic

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:34.080
into a dynamically
generated signature.

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.570
The result of this analysis
is presented immediately

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:38.900
as part of the original alert.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:42.560
We also take it a step further
and provide a proposed Cloud

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:44.510
Armor rule that can
mitigate the attack

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:47.180
traffic with minimal
to no collateral damage

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:49.340
to the good baseline traffic.

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:52.550
Adaptive Protection provides
near-real-time attack detection

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:54.920
with immediately
actionable alerts,

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:58.190
saving your incident responders
critical minutes or hours

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:01.760
of time-consuming log
analysis, triage, and other IR

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:05.570
activities, allowing you to
safely and efficiently deliver

00:07:05.570 --> 00:07:06.455
reliable services.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:12.940
This is the main Adaptive
Protection dashboard.

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:15.330
Here, you can see
incoming traffic patterns

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:17.100
for all of your
services protected

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:19.350
by Adaptive
Protection as well as

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:22.620
the list of attack alerts
detected over the selected time

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:23.940
horizon.

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:26.720
When we click on
one of the alerts,

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:28.910
we see a much more
detailed view of the attack

00:07:28.910 --> 00:07:31.790
and the results of our machine
learning-powered analysis.

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:34.190
We present the
machine-determined confidence

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:37.670
level in our findings, the
size and shape of the attack,

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:39.950
as well as the attack signature.

00:07:39.950 --> 00:07:41.390
For this attack
in particular, we

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:43.700
have identified and
presented a unique signature

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:47.240
across the source region code,
remote host, and target host

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:48.450
attributes.

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:51.530
We also propose a rule that can
be deployed in near real time

00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:53.720
to mitigate the attack
as it's occurring.

00:07:56.610 --> 00:07:59.610
Because of the sensitivity
of our detection models,

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:02.460
alerts are frequently generated
while an attack is still

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:05.970
ramping up, allowing you to
respond before it has a chance

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:08.830
to impact your services.

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:10.930
When the operator
clicks Apply, they

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:13.690
can see the proposed rule, as
well as additional guidance

00:08:13.690 --> 00:08:17.410
from Adaptive Protection, to aid
in understanding and assessing

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:21.080
the expected impact of
the proposed mitigation.

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:24.470
Mitigations can also be deployed
in a logging-only or preview

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:28.570
mode to validate
effectiveness and safety.

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:31.120
Specifically, you can
see the expected impact

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:34.179
of the proposed rule on
the good and bad traffic.

00:08:34.179 --> 00:08:36.460
In this case, the
proposed rule is

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:39.520
expected to impact 0%
of the good traffic

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:43.360
from the baseline while
blocking about all or nearly all

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:45.100
of the incoming attack.

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:47.650
So we are providing
an end-to-end solution

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:51.550
to detect and mitigate attacks
with near-real-time actionable

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:55.300
alerts containing dynamically
generated mitigations, which

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:58.880
are narrowly targeted for
just the malicious traffic.

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:01.670
This insight not only
saves incident responders

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:05.090
critical time in reacting and
mounting an effective defense,

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:06.770
but also provides
the information

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:09.920
to aid triage efforts and
help explain why we consider

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:11.450
the traffic suspicious.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:17.660
Next, we're proud to announce
the preview release of Cloud

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:19.850
Armor Bot Management
with a deep integration

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:23.240
with reCAPTCHA Enterprise, which
is Google Cloud's enterprise

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:26.930
version of the leading bot
and fraud protection product.

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:30.230
While customers can use these
products together already,

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:32.360
we wanted to simplify
the deployment process

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:36.200
and provide a more integrated
and scalable experience.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:39.260
ReCAPTCHA Enterprise provides
a world-class bot detection

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:42.050
mechanism developed
from and continuously

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:44.750
learning from a
globally distributed

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:47.480
multi-million
website install base.

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:49.790
With this deep integration,
Cloud Armor customers

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:52.790
can now leverage reCAPTCHA
to detect and block

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:56.480
bot traffic within their
Cloud Armor security policies.

00:09:56.480 --> 00:09:58.970
All of this occurs at the
edge of Google's network,

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:01.700
far upstream of your
protected workloads,

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:04.400
allowing us to leverage the
global scale of our network

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:07.520
to mitigate bot threats without
impacting the performance

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:09.380
of your applications.

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:11.810
Critically, with the
integrated solution,

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:13.430
Cloud Armor and
reCAPTCHA Enterprise

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:15.470
can protect customer
applications

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:18.770
without requiring server-side
changes on the application

00:10:18.770 --> 00:10:22.040
to decipher and enforce
bot assessments.

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:24.260
You can configure Cloud
Armor security policies

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:27.020
in several ways to
leverage this integration.

00:10:27.020 --> 00:10:28.880
You can configure
any rule action

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:30.500
to serve the
reCAPTCHA challenge,

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:33.320
forcing suspicious end
users to solve the puzzle

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:34.997
before being allowed through.

00:10:34.997 --> 00:10:37.580
Additionally, you can instrument
your client-side applications

00:10:37.580 --> 00:10:41.120
with the reCAPTCHA library
for seamless and silent bot

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:44.480
assessments and then have Cloud
Armor decipher and enforce

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:47.720
the assessment at the edge.

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:49.610
In addition to the
reCAPTCHA integration,

00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:52.370
Cloud Armor Bot Management
introduces an enhanced set

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:55.610
of capabilities designed to
help you deploy an effective bot

00:10:55.610 --> 00:10:57.230
management strategy.

00:10:57.230 --> 00:10:59.510
These capabilities include
additional rule actions

00:10:59.510 --> 00:11:01.790
to redirect traffic,
as well as the ability

00:11:01.790 --> 00:11:04.730
to insert a custom header
into a suspicious request,

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:07.880
but otherwise allow it to
reach your application.

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:11.120
Customers use this custom
header to tag suspicious traffic

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:13.970
for alternative processing
within their workloads,

00:11:13.970 --> 00:11:16.040
like sending to
a honeypot or not

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:18.500
completing a potentially
fraudulent transaction

00:11:18.500 --> 00:11:22.850
without notifying the
bot that it was detected.

00:11:22.850 --> 00:11:25.790
We're also proud to share
that one of our most highly

00:11:25.790 --> 00:11:27.890
anticipated features,
rate limiting,

00:11:27.890 --> 00:11:29.960
is now available in preview.

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:32.000
With Cloud Armor
per-client rate limiting,

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:34.970
users can ensure that
no client exceeds

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:37.850
a pre-configured threshold
through two new actions

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:41.130
for any existing or
new Cloud Armor rule.

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:42.750
With the throttle
action, requests

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:45.720
from individual clients will be
dropped when the client begins

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:49.140
exceeding the prescribed rate
to ensure that, on average,

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:50.880
the client's
request volume stays

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:53.440
within the configured threshold.

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:55.870
Similarly, the
rate-based ban action

00:11:55.870 --> 00:12:00.220
aims to punish abuse of clients
by temporarily banning them

00:12:00.220 --> 00:12:04.440
for a configured amount of time
if they exceed your threshold.

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:06.560
And clients can be
identified by their source

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:10.310
IP, X-Forwarded-For header,
or any arbitrary header,

00:12:10.310 --> 00:12:13.610
like a session
identifier or a user ID.

00:12:13.610 --> 00:12:15.860
Cloud Armor rate limiting
is an effective means

00:12:15.860 --> 00:12:18.800
to detect and thwart
high-volume DDoS attempts,

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:21.020
mitigate credential
stuffing attacks,

00:12:21.020 --> 00:12:24.230
all while ensuring the
availability and reliability

00:12:24.230 --> 00:12:27.180
of your workloads.

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:29.790
Finally, Cloud Armor
edge security policies

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:32.820
are available in preview to
provide Cloud Armor protection

00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:37.890
in front of Cloud CDN cache or
Google Cloud Storage buckets.

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:39.690
The new edge
security policy types

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:43.410
can be applied to protected
services in parallel

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:46.230
with the existing back-end
security policies.

00:12:46.230 --> 00:12:48.720
The new policy types
are stored, evaluated,

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:51.360
and enforced even further
upstream in our edge

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:54.960
infrastructure before a request
can be served from the CDN's

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:57.820
cache or from a GCS bucket.

00:12:57.820 --> 00:13:01.210
Requests that make it through
the edge security policy that

00:13:01.210 --> 00:13:04.450
cannot be served from cache
are still evaluated by any

00:13:04.450 --> 00:13:07.780
configured back-end security
policy for full Layer 7

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:11.140
filtering and WAF before it is
proxied back to your protected

00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:13.270
application or origins.

00:13:13.270 --> 00:13:15.970
Edge security policies
support IP and geo-filtering

00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:19.540
to filter out unwelcome end
users or geographies in order

00:13:19.540 --> 00:13:23.890
to comply with security
compliance, export controls,

00:13:23.890 --> 00:13:26.140
or licensing agreements.

00:13:26.140 --> 00:13:28.630
While delivering these much
anticipated capabilities,

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:30.520
we are continuing
to invest heavily

00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:33.910
into expanding Cloud Armor
scope and depth of protection

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:36.470
to help protect your
applications and services.

00:13:36.470 --> 00:13:38.080
So stay tuned.

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:40.510
Now I'll hand it back
to Peter to tell you

00:13:40.510 --> 00:13:43.130
about more great developments
across our network security

00:13:43.130 --> 00:13:43.630
products.

00:13:46.158 --> 00:13:47.200
PETER BLUM: Thanks, Emil.

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:49.390
It's great to hear about
all the good stuff that's

00:13:49.390 --> 00:13:51.190
happening with Cloud Armor.

00:13:51.190 --> 00:13:53.890
Next, I'd like to get into
what's new and innovative

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:55.180
with network protection.

00:13:55.180 --> 00:13:58.420
I'm going to cover innovations
in Cloud Firewall, Cloud

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:01.450
NAT, and Cloud IDS.

00:14:01.450 --> 00:14:03.580
So let's start with
Cloud Firewall.

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:06.070
This is our integrated
virtual firewall service

00:14:06.070 --> 00:14:09.670
for ingress, egress, and inside
the cloud network protection.

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:12.430
It's fully integrated
with Google's SDN fabric.

00:14:12.430 --> 00:14:14.500
And the firewall
policy enforcement

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:17.020
is distributed
across that fabric.

00:14:17.020 --> 00:14:19.360
And to make firewall
policies dynamic,

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:22.360
we allow for easier
microsegmentation

00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:25.720
and provide a shift to a
zero-trust workload protection

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:29.140
posture by using network
tags and service accounts.

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:32.380
This removes the need for
encoding static IP addresses

00:14:32.380 --> 00:14:35.710
into firewall rules and
makes protection dynamic.

00:14:35.710 --> 00:14:38.980
The example I like to give is
you can add new microservices

00:14:38.980 --> 00:14:39.910
with a tag.

00:14:39.910 --> 00:14:42.610
And they'll automatically get
the established protection

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:43.480
rules.

00:14:43.480 --> 00:14:45.580
And in terms of
recent innovations,

00:14:45.580 --> 00:14:47.440
we have two great additions.

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:49.480
First is Firewall
Insights, which

00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:52.690
provides information that helps
operators learn about which

00:14:52.690 --> 00:14:56.680
firewall rules are used,
never used, and overshadowed

00:14:56.680 --> 00:14:58.660
by other, more broad, rules.

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:01.510
This helps identify
misconfigurations,

00:15:01.510 --> 00:15:05.530
attacks, and allows tightening
controls for better security.

00:15:05.530 --> 00:15:08.860
And then we just released a
new capability in preview here

00:15:08.860 --> 00:15:11.320
that highlights rules
that have perhaps

00:15:11.320 --> 00:15:14.500
over-granted permissions,
which is a great opportunity

00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:17.080
to tighten up your
firewall rules.

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:19.840
Next, we released
hierarchical firewall policies

00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:23.080
earlier this year, which
allows creating and enforcing

00:15:23.080 --> 00:15:25.510
a consistent set of
firewall policies

00:15:25.510 --> 00:15:28.160
across an entire organization.

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:30.610
This improves protection
and simplifies

00:15:30.610 --> 00:15:32.680
complex, large deployments.

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:35.260
The great thing is that once
you set up your organization's

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:37.390
standard set of
firewall policies,

00:15:37.390 --> 00:15:41.290
any new projects added
automatically get that policy.

00:15:41.290 --> 00:15:43.660
But it still allows
for local overrides

00:15:43.660 --> 00:15:46.630
deeper within the
organization when required.

00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:49.360
We find it's really
helping customers simplify

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:51.670
their firewall
configurations by reducing

00:15:51.670 --> 00:15:55.270
the number of firewall rules
needed across an organization.

00:15:57.780 --> 00:15:59.300
Next up is Cloud NAT.

00:15:59.300 --> 00:16:02.960
This is our integrated network
address translation service.

00:16:02.960 --> 00:16:05.420
Like our firewall, it's
built into the fabric

00:16:05.420 --> 00:16:07.370
and provides
security and privacy

00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:09.470
for outbound connections
with the ability

00:16:09.470 --> 00:16:13.040
to use static or
auto-allocated IPs.

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:15.500
And like our firewall,
it's built into the network

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:17.420
fabric versus a single gateway.

00:16:17.420 --> 00:16:20.480
So you can scale it up
to thousands of VMs.

00:16:20.480 --> 00:16:22.460
And in terms of
recent innovations,

00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:25.850
we have two great additions
in preview now at the show.

00:16:25.850 --> 00:16:27.950
The first is NAT
rules, which allow

00:16:27.950 --> 00:16:30.830
for tighter control over
which external IPs are

00:16:30.830 --> 00:16:33.230
used for different
internal requesters.

00:16:33.230 --> 00:16:35.030
For example, you
can ensure a service

00:16:35.030 --> 00:16:38.750
making requests out over NAT
always uses the same public IP

00:16:38.750 --> 00:16:39.860
address.

00:16:39.860 --> 00:16:42.380
And then we introduced a
new capability in preview

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:44.540
called dynamic port allocation.

00:16:44.540 --> 00:16:48.500
This allows for higher scale
by automatically optimizing NAT

00:16:48.500 --> 00:16:50.870
port utilization in real time.

00:16:50.870 --> 00:16:53.330
Previously, customers
had to manually configure

00:16:53.330 --> 00:16:55.220
port allocations across VMs.

00:16:55.220 --> 00:16:57.050
And they were generally
over-allocated.

00:16:57.050 --> 00:17:02.160
Now the system does it for
you on its own automatically.

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:05.790
And we just recently announced
our new Cloud IDS product,

00:17:05.790 --> 00:17:07.920
which provides for
managed network threat

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:10.349
detection with high
levels of security

00:17:10.349 --> 00:17:12.569
efficacy and low noise.

00:17:12.569 --> 00:17:14.410
It's quick and easy to deploy.

00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:16.650
It's managed, scaled,
and operated by Google,

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:19.800
with high performance and
availability all built in.

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:21.210
It's super easy to set up.

00:17:21.210 --> 00:17:22.950
With a few clicks,
you just select

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:25.680
what you want to monitor,
which types of monitoring

00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:29.310
you want to enable, and then
the logging and telemetry

00:17:29.310 --> 00:17:31.710
is all integrated into
all our own systems,

00:17:31.710 --> 00:17:34.260
like Cloud Logging, Chronicle.

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:36.840
And all the alerts flow to SCC.

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:40.350
We can also send the alerts and
telemetry to third-party SIEM

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:42.438
and SOAR products as well.

00:17:42.438 --> 00:17:44.730
And then we're super excited
to share that we partnered

00:17:44.730 --> 00:17:47.070
with our friends at
Palo Alto Networks

00:17:47.070 --> 00:17:48.840
to embed their
industry-leading threat

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:51.550
detection technologies
in the product.

00:17:51.550 --> 00:17:54.120
So this ensures we have
industry-leading detection

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:56.190
capabilities, both
in terms of breadth

00:17:56.190 --> 00:17:58.020
of coverage and efficacy.

00:17:58.020 --> 00:17:59.850
And it's all backed
by their expert,

00:17:59.850 --> 00:18:02.700
production-proven technologies
and world-class threat

00:18:02.700 --> 00:18:05.340
intelligence teams,
but all nicely

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:09.150
integrated into Google Cloud,
sold and supported by us.

00:18:09.150 --> 00:18:12.240
In fact, I wanted you to hear
from our GM of IaS, Sachin

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:16.200
Gupta, and Palo Alto
Networks's SVP, Anand Oswal,

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:18.675
more about the collaboration
that led to this product.

